{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**Original key (E‑A‑B)**  \r\n\r\n- **E major** : (E, G♯, B)  \r\n- **A major** : (A, C♯, E)  \r\n- **B major** : (B, D♯, F♯)  \r\n\r\n**Transposed down a whole‑step to D (D‑G‑A)**  \r\n\r\n- **D major** : (D, F♯, A)  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/4.2/3.3/2.1/2)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n- **G major** : (G, B, D)  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/6.2/5.0/4.0/3.0/2.0/1.3)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n- **A major** : (A, C♯, E)  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/5.0/4.2/3.2/2.2/1.0)\r\n```  \r\n\r\nPlay the three chords in the order **D – G – A** and the song will sit a whole step lower, making the vocal range more comfortable.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":4597,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":3,"id":"f-1","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"song is E A B but i cant sing that high, need it in D","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":0}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**Yes – the basic E‑major and C‑major chords share one note: E.**  \r\n\r\n| Chord | Notes (root‑third‑fifth) |\r\n|-------|--------------------------|\r\n| **E major** | (E G♯ B) |\r\n| **C major** | (C E G) |\r\n\r\nThe only pitch that appears in both sets is **E**.\r\n\r\nIf you use the **E minor** triad (E G B) instead, it would share two notes with C major: **E** and **G**.\r\n\r\nBelow are open‑position voicings for the two major triads, with exact fret positions shown in VexTab.\r\n\r\n**E major (open)** – notes E G♯ B  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/6.0/5.2/4.2/3.1/2.0/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**C major (open)** – notes C E G  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/5.3/4.2/3.0/2.1/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo, when you’re voice‑leading from C major to E major (or the reverse), the **E** can be held as a common tone, giving a smooth, connected sound. If you prefer a richer common‑tone relationship, try swapping C major for **C add9** (C‑E‑G‑D) or using **E minor** to share both **E** and **G**.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2327,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":2,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":3,"id":"f-12","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"do E and C have any notes in common at all","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":2}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The interval from **G up to E** is a **major sixth** (9 semitones). In interval terminology you’d simply call it a “sixth,” and specifying the quality makes it a **major sixth**.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":983,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":2,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-20","kind":null,"label":"skill.interval","match":null,"prompt":"from G up to E what do you call that distance","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":2}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**D major diatonic chords**\r\n\r\n| Roman numeral | Triad (notes) | 7‑chord (notes) |\r\n|---------------|---------------|-----------------|\r\n| I | D (F♯ A) | Dmaj7 (D F♯ A C♯) |\r\n| ii | Em (E G B) | Em7 (E G B D) |\r\n| iii | F♯m (F♯ A C♯) | F♯m7 (F♯ A C♯ E) |\r\n| IV | G (G B D) | Gmaj7 (G B D F♯) |\r\n| V | A (A C♯ E) | A7 (A C♯ E G) |\r\n| vi | Bm (B D F♯) | Bm7 (B D F♯ A) |\r\n| vii° | C♯dim (C♯ E G) | C♯m7♭5 (C♯ E G B) |\r\n\r\n**Common diatonic extensions**\r\n\r\n- **Dadd9** (D F♯ A E)  \r\n- **Dsus2** (D E A)  \r\n- **Dsus4** (D G A)  \r\n- **Em9** (E G B D F♯)  \r\n- **F♯m11** (F♯ A C♯ E B)  \r\n- **G6** (G B D E)  \r\n- **A13** (A C♯ E G B F♯)  \r\n- **Bm9** (B D F♯ A C♯)\r\n\r\n**Secondary dominants that fit naturally in D major**\r\n\r\n| Function | Chord (notes) |\r\n|----------|---------------|\r\n| V⁄V (secondary dominant of A) | **E7** (E G♯ B D) |\r\n| V⁄ii (secondary dominant of Em) | **B7** (B D♯ F♯ A) |\r\n| V⁄vi (secondary dominant of Bm) | **F♯7** (F♯ A♯ C♯ E) |\r\n\r\nThese chords all contain notes drawn from the D major scale (or its closely related altered tones for secondary dominants) and can be used freely in progressions within the key.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2730,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":17,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":1,"id":"f-28","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"chords that work in D major?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":["Dmaj7 (D F# A C#)","Gmaj7 (G B D F#)","A7 (A C# E G)","Dsus2 (D E A)","Dsus4 (D G A)","G6 (G B D E)","E7 (E G# B D)","B7 (B D# F# A)","F#7 (F# A# C# E)"],"verdict":"F","verified":13}
