{"agent":"skill.keyidentification","answer":"The four‑chord loop Am – F – C – G fits perfectly in the key of C major (or its relative minor, A minor). Both keys contain all four chords diatonically, so they are tied. You can tell which key the song’s using by listening for the \"home\" feeling – a resolution to C (the bright, happy tonic) suggests C major, while a resolution to Am (the darker, more introspective tonic) points to A minor.\r\n\r\n**Roman‑numeral analysis in C major**\r\n- Am → vi (minor submediant)\r\n- F  → IV (major subdominant)\r\n- C  → I  (major tonic)\r\n- G  → V  (major dominant)\r\n\r\n**C major scale (the natural notes)**: C D E F G A B.\r\n\r\n**Transposing the whole progression up a whole step (two semitones)** moves each chord’s root up two frets:\r\n- Am → **Bm** (B D F#)\r\n- F  → **G**  (G B D)\r\n- C  → **D**  (D F# A)\r\n- G  → **A**  (A C# E)\r\n\r\nThese new chords belong to the key of D major (or its relative minor, B minor). In D major the functions become:\r\n- Bm → vi\r\n- G  → IV\r\n- D  → I\r\n- A  → V\r\n\r\nSo, up a whole step the progression is **Bm – G – D – A**, with the notes shown in parentheses.\r\n\r\nHappy strumming!","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":3926,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":5,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-73","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"put Am F C G up a whole step pls","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":5}
