{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**Chord name:** D minor augmented‑fifth – written **D m(♯5)** (sometimes labeled **D°♯5**).  \r\n\r\n**Notes:** D – F – A♯ (A♯ ≡ B♭).  \r\n\r\n**Typical harmonic function:** It acts as a **leading‑tone chord** (vii°) to the key centre a half‑step above – most commonly resolving to **E♭ major or E♭ minor**. The raised fifth (A♯) gives it extra tension, so it’s often used as a chromatic passing or embellishing chord that wants to move to an Eb‑based harmony.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":5731,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"m-37","kind":null,"label":"ambiguous","match":null,"prompt":"What is the name and function of the D-F-A♯ chord in this guitar score?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"music-se","source_author":"Tikhon","source_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source_link":"https://music.stackexchange.com/questions/144196/what-is-the-name-and-function-of-the-d-f-a%e2%99%af-chord-in-this-guitar-score","unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":0}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**D Harmonic Minor – Diatonic chords**\r\n\r\n| Scale degree | Roman | Chord (notes) | Typical guitar shape |\r\n|--------------|-------|---------------|----------------------|\r\n| i            | Dm    | D F A | `vextab` |\r\n| ii°          | E°    | E G B♭ | `vextab` |\r\n| III+         | F+    | F A C# | `vextab` |\r\n| iv           | Gm    | G B♭ D | `vextab` |\r\n| V            | A     | A C# E | `vextab` |\r\n| VI           | B♭    | B♭ D F | `vextab` |\r\n| vii°         | C#°   | C# E G | `vextab` |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Guitar voicings (all in open‑position where possible)\r\n\r\n**i – Dm (D F A)**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (1/1.1/2.3/3.2/4.0/5.x/6.x)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n**ii° – E° (E G B♭)**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/1.0/2.0/3.1/4.x/5.x/6.x)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n**III+ – F+ (F A C#)**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/2.1/3.2/4.3/5.x/6.x/1.x)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n**iv – Gm (G B♭ D)**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/3.3/4.0/5.1/6.3/1.x/2.x)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n**V – A (A C# E)**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/1.2/2.2/3.2/4.2/5.x/6.x)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n**VI – B♭ (B♭ D F)**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (1/1.3/2.3/3.3/4.1/5.x/6.x)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n**vii° – C#° (C# E G)**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (4/4.4/5.2/6.4/1.x/2.x/3.x)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Common progressions in D Harmonic Minor  \r\n\r\n1. **i – iv – V – i** → Dm → Gm → A → Dm  \r\n2. **i – VI – III+ – V** → Dm → B♭ → F+ → A  \r\n3. **i – ii° – V – i** → Dm → E° → A → Dm (E° functions as a leading‑tone chord to A)\r\n\r\nFeel free to mix these shapes, add sevenths (e.g., DmΔ7 = D F A C#) or substitute the V with an **A7** (A C# E G) for extra tension before resolving back to i.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":3793,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":12,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":3,"id":"m-81","kind":null,"label":"ambiguous","match":null,"prompt":"D Harmonic Minor or something else","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"music-se","source_author":"Justin Wardrobe","source_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source_link":"https://music.stackexchange.com/questions/144031/d-harmonic-minor-or-something-else","unchecked":["F+ | F A C#","F+ (F A C#)","A7 (A C# E G)"],"verdict":"T","verified":12}
